{
  "title": "Hammer Toe",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/foot-and-ankle/7014/hammer-toe",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Mar 17 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Hammer Toe is a lesser toe deformity characterized by PIP flexion, DIP extension and MTP slight extension.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made clinically with the presence of a rigid or flexible lesser toe with PIP flexion, DIP extension and MTP slight extension.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is a trial of nonoperative management with shoe modification. Surgical management is indicated for progressive deformity, fixed contractures, and dorsal toe ulcerations.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**most common deformity of lesser toes**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "more common in older women",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
